  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Upper level troughing essentially sitting \ndirectly atop the western Great Lakes early this afternoon. \nAttendant area of surface low pressure now centered downstream over \nQuebec. Initial cold front crossed last night, with a secondary cold \nfront beginning to make headway into far northern/western sections \nof the forecast area. This boundary will cross through the remainder \nof the afternoon into early evening before exiting to the southeast. \nCold air advection to be the rule tonight into Friday before higher \nsurface pressure and low-level warm advection firmly take over later \nFriday afternoon into Friday night. The weekend through much of\nnext week look mainly dry, although an active northern stream \nwill allow several waves to trek by to our north, the first as \nearly as Sunday with additional Tuesday and again Thursday. \n\nForecast Details: Primary focus this afternoon through Friday \ncontinues to revolve around increasingly gusty winds and continued \nrain showers, which may eventually mix with or change over to all \nsnow for some locations by later tonight into Friday morning. \n\nWinds will be in the process of turning out of northwest post cold \nfrontal passage this afternoon and early evening. Latest trends \nsupport increasing gustiness behind this frontal passage with \nhighest probabilities for localized gusts of 35+ mph across far \nnorthwest lower MI and near Whitefish Bay in the eastern U.P. While \nthe timeframe for strongest winds is between 22z-08z, it'll remain \nbreezy for much of northern MI pretty much through the day Friday \nwith gusts of 20-30 mph common. Wind chills at commute/bus stop time \nFriday morning expected to range from the mid-20s to low 30s area-\nwide.\n\nPeriods of numerous rain showers across parts of the area are \nexpected to remain the rule at times through the remainder of the \nday, tonight and into Friday. Showers heading into tonight/Friday \naided by lingering synoptic support/moisture combined with \nincreasing over-lake instability as cold air advection ramps up. H8 \ntemps favored to bottom out between -4.5 to -6 C by late tonight \ninto Friday morning with a rain/snow mix or change over to all wet \nsnow becoming likely across parts of the forecast area -- primarily \nsections of eastern U.P. and the interior higher terrain of northern \nlower. Surface temps a little trickier with latest trends favoring \nthe coldest spots falling to 33-36F during this time frame, but \ncertainly in the realm of possibilities for locations that see \nheavier/steadier precipitation to drop a touch cooler than that. \nNone the less, any accumulation of wet snow should be minor (~0.5\" \nor less) and primarily be limited to cooler and/or elevated surfaces \ngiven ground temps should be sufficiently warm to melt most of what \nfalls. That said, latest REFS probs of 35-45% exist for 1\"+ of \naccum. by 18z Friday, primarily near and north of M-28 in Chippewa \nCounty.\n\nLingering synoptic support exits through Friday afternoon with any \nrain/snow mix turning back to all rain before the vast majority of \nprecip exits by Friday evening. Perhaps a few lingering lake induced \nrain showers in north flow favored lake effect areas, but overall, a \nmuch quieter Friday night anticipated. \n\nAs was alluded to above, latest trends support less active \nconditions return to northern MI this weekend, and potentially \nthrough much of the remainder of the forcast period. That said, \nconfidence decreases heading towards the middle of next week as \naforementioned active northern jet may buckle enough to send another \nshot of cooler temperatures and lake enhanced precip into the area. \nHigh temps Saturday in the 40s and low 50s area-wide with more 50s \nfavored Sunday through at least Tuesday.",
